---
description: Guidelines for writing code comments
alwaysApply: true
---

Err on the side of commenting in more places. Keep each comment concise.

- Comment (with `/** */`) every exported function and React component.
- Comment (with `/** */`) on component props, function inputs, internal state
  variables and callbacks within a component/hook that might
  need a bit more explaining
- Comment with `//` inside long functions to explain what it's doing, or above
  code to explain why we're doing something, what's unusual, etc. if not obvious
  from the code.

## What to write

Write **why**, how to use it, or an example with real data or real-world uses
— not a restatement of the next line.

```ts
// Bad: restates the function name
// Gets the place block before first place in this section
const placeBeforeSection = getPlaceBlockBeforeFirstPlaceInSection({...});

// Good: why we need this value
// We want to show distances to the first place on this day at the top of
// the day.
const placeBeforeSection = getPlaceBlockBeforeFirstPlaceInSection({...});
```

- Include lots of links: link to the source for workarounds, API docs,
  StackOverflow, etc. A publicly accessible agent/chat link may be included
  when useful.
- For unintuitive or hacky code, say what you're doing and why it's safe and
  needed vs. the more obvious code
- If two sites must stay aligned, put a `Keep in sync` comment on both
  sides that names the other file or symbol
  - When adding another location, update every existing comment to list all
    current locations
  - When more than two locations stay aligned, list them as bullets
  - In `package.json`, add a `__…-comment__` string next to a script or
    dependency when it should stay aligned with related code changes. For
    dependency notes, use the `__dependencyComments__` object when appropriate.
  - On copied numbers that go stale (ratings, list prices), add a
    last-updated date
- If adding a TODO, always link to the Notion task that should later implement
  it; include a spec number too when one exists.
- Don't restate the signature or parameters, or anything you can infer from the
  name
- Describe what this value does in this file. Don't list which parents pass
  it, or which sources currently exist. Those lists go stale.
- Don't describe how this version differs from an earlier draft, branch, or
  conversation ("no longer doing X", "master never had that"). Explain the
  current code on its own.
- Read comments out loud: is this how you would say it to a (human) colleague?
  If not, rewrite so it's easier to read. A few lines is enough. If a comment
  is turning into a paragraph, shorten it or split it next to the lines it
  explains.

## When to delete

Delete a comment that only restates the next line, the identifier, or the
function name, and has no why to add. Prefer no comment over a restatement.

After extracting a function, drop the leftover comment if the new name
already says the same thing.

## Placement

Prefer several small comments placed next to the specific code they explain
over one large comment block (e.g. at the top of a file or test) that covers
many unrelated points at once. A reader looking at one piece of code shouldn't
have to scroll away to find the explanation.

Bad: one big comment up front covering several unrelated points

```typescript
// This test checks angle calculations for cardinal directions. 0 degrees
// means no rotation, so the point stays the same. 90 degrees rotates a
// quarter turn, swapping x and y. 180 degrees flips both signs.
it.each([...])('rotates $angle', ({ angle, expected }) => {
  expect(rotate(point, angle)).toEqual(expected);
});

// If the multiplier is more than 1, this is good, but doesn't need attention,
// so we just log it. If the multiplier is less than 1, this might be a bad sign
// and we should investigate, so we alert. If there's no change, it's
// unfortunate we're not increasing, but it's no big deal, so we just log it.
if (multiplier > 1) logIncrease();
else if (multiplier < 1) alertDecrease();
else logChecked();
```

Good: each explanation lives next to the case it explains

```typescript
it.each([
  // No rotation: the point is unchanged.
  { angle: 0, expected: point },
  // Quarter turn: x and y swap.
  { angle: 90, expected: swapped },
  // Half turn: both signs flip.
  { angle: 180, expected: negated },
])('rotates $angle', ({ angle, expected }) => {
  expect(rotate(point, angle)).toEqual(expected);
});

// This is good, but doesn't need attention, so we just log it
if (multiplier > 1) logIncrease();
// This might be a bad sign and we should investigate, so we alert
else if (multiplier < 1) alertDecrease();
// It's unfortunate we're not increasing, but it's no big deal, so we just log it
else logChecked();
```

## Match the code

After you change code, re-read comments near the edits (and any files,
functions, or types the comment refers to).

If the comment is outdated, update or delete it.

## Tone

- Casual and conversational.
- Use we, I, and contractions (I'm, isn't).
- Use short, simple sentences.
- Prefer active voice.
- Avoid double negatives and use positive framing.
- Use plain words that any engineer can follow, not jargon for people already
  familiar with the code.
- Use bullets and numbering to break up long paragraphs.
  If a comment is more than 4 lines of solid prose:
  - Option 1: break into paragraphs
  - Option 2: have parts be bullets/numbered bullets

Avoid AI-isms (load-bearing, drift) and long words when a short one will work.

Example:

```ts
// Bad: step list, negative framing, says what caller already knows
/**
 * Preprocessed import parts: skip Spaces put when the same user already has
 * a `MediaImport` row for this input hash; otherwise upload originals then run
 * `callback`. Deletes fresh uploads when `callback` throws.
 */
export async function uploadImportFromMediaFilesFromBuffers<T>({}) {}

// Good: what does it do, context with examples. Longer, but simpler sentences
// and flow
// Upload files that a user had uploaded for us to import into a trip and that
// we had pre-processed (e.g., by resizing images), then call a callback with
// paths to the uploaded files.
//
// This function will avoid re-uploading files that it seems like we'd already
// uploaded.
//
// Also, if the callback throws, we'll delete the files so we don't leave them
// orphaned.
export async function uploadImportFromMediaFilesFromBuffers<T>({}) {}

// Bad: lots of long sentences that use passive voice and jargon (e.g.,
// "overview copy")
/**
 * Minimum height so the slideshow card fills leftover height at default text
 * size and stays tall enough for the screenshot when overview copy grows.
 * Measured in the full-height sheet on a typical phone; bump and check a
 * device if the overview copy or screenshots drift.
 */
const minSlideshowCardHeight = 300;

// Better: shorter sentences, plainer words
/**
 * We want to make sure that the slideshow card with the image is always at
 * least this tall. That way, even if the text above it grows, the image
 * doesn't get shrunk down too much such that it's hard to see.
 *
 * Keep in sync with screenshot dimensions: if e.g., we switch to landscape
 * screenshots, change this.
 */
const minSlideshowCardHeight = 300;

// Note: using `/suggest-eslint-or-cursor-rules`? If you see other bad comments,
// feel free to add them here along with the corrected comment.
```

## Format

Wrap comments at 80 characters (including leading indent: the text might be
shorter than 80 characters if very indented)

- `/** */` above declarations: functions, types, props, state
- `//` for inline notes, comments above JSX props
- `{/* */}` for single-line JSX comments
- Multi-line comments inside JSX:

```tsx
{
  // This is a multi-line comment. Wrap it at 80 characters
  // including the leading whitespace.
}
<SomeComponent
  // Here's some comments above a certain prop
  someProp={123}
>
```

If a comment is more than about four lines of prose, split it into bullets
or numbered bullets.
